Find where {{ model }} under {{ framework }} would run faster if a chain of its ops became one
kernel. Each kernel worth optimizing on its own already was; what is left is what only a
fusion removes: the launches between the ops, and the intermediates one writes to device
memory and the next reads back. An op at its bandwidth bound, or one inside PyTorch with no
kernel repository to edit, has nothing else left to give.

What the run established, all under {{ run_dir }}:
 - {{ capture }}: every op the model ran, with its shapes, `call_site` (the module that
   called it) and device time, grouped into workloads. Ops are aggregated per shape and call
   site, not in order: `raw/unitrace_launches.json` is the measured run's kernel launches in
   device order, and the model's source shows which op feeds which.
 - names.json: each op's kernel and the repository it lives in; analysis.json and
   kernels.yaml: which kernels were optimized and why the others were not.
 - results/SUMMARY.md and results/summary.json: what optimizing each kernel won, and its patch.
{%- if stacked %}
 - The repositories with those winning patches applied and committed, which a fusion is
   built on and its patch applies over:
{%- for origin, clone in stacked.items() %}
   {{ clone }} (from {{ origin }})
{%- endfor %}
{%- endif %}
The kernel repositories are in {{ repos }}.

Propose the chains that are worth it: ops that run back to back on the same data, every
time, where one kernel can do what they do -- a residual add before a norm, an activation
after a gated product, an elementwise op on a kernel's output. For each, write
refs/<name>_pytorch.py: the chain as PyTorch -- a `Model` whose forward does what the ops do,
in order, at the dtypes the capture shows (say in its docstring whether the model rounds
between them), and `get_init_inputs()` / `get_inputs()` taking between them exactly the dims of
the anchor's workload in {{ capture }} and `dtype` (the torch dtype it runs at) as keyword
arguments, every other size a constant read from the capture. Its docstring names each op it covers and where the framework calls it
(file:line): that is the scope the optimizing session locates and copies as its baseline.

The fusion is built and optimized as a kernel is: in `repo` (the repository its kernel
goes in -- a stacked one above when its ops' kernels were patched there), by an Xe-Forge
session scored on the anchor's shapes, against the optimized chain. A fusion that changes
an op's interface or its call sites needs the repositories wired to it as
`integration_repos` and is delivered as serving-only.

Run `{{ check_cmd }}` until it prints PASS; it ranks what passes by the time the chain's ops
take after the kernels' wins. Then write {{ answer }}: {"fusions": [{"name": "<identifier>",
"repo": "<path>", "dsl": "<one of {{ dsls | join(', ') }}>", "ops": ["<op id in the
capture>", ...], "anchor": "<the op whose workload sets the shapes>", "reference":
"refs/<name>_pytorch.py", "integration_repos": ["<path>", ...], "why": "<what is fused and
what that removes, one line>"}, ...]}, or {"fusions": [], "why": "<why none is worth it>"}.
